Apply order 0024 against mariopartyrd/partyboard 98ac07fd1e8f7ab938bbb6fe75789fad99419010 (CC0-1.0; ADR 0009 section 5).
m402, m405, m429, m432, m441, m450: no VECAdd into the arg0 pointer's own stack slot, on MPT_PC
Applied in order by runtimes/gc_wii/mp4/CMakeLists.txt with `git apply`.

Six minigames share a movement helper, fn_1_1FC4(Vec *arg0, ...), that ends with
VECAdd((Vec *)&arg0, &sp18, (Vec *)&arg0). Like Goooooooal's hit test (patch 0023), it adds to the
bytes of the pointer, not to the vector, and writes 12 bytes over the pointer's stack slot. On the
console that slot and its neighbours are dead when the function returns, so the game is unaffected.
In the 64-bit module the slot is 8 bytes and /GS checks the frame on return, so the overrun is a
fail-fast waiting for the right stack layout: patch 0023's fail-fast had the same cause. None of the
six has failed yet (Slime Time and Team Treasure Trek ran in P1-17 and M4-01). On MPT_PC the call
is left out, which keeps the console's behaviour: the call never changed *arg0.

diff --git a/src/REL/m402Dll/main.c b/src/REL/m402Dll/main.c
index badd9d3..5545d87 100644
--- a/src/REL/m402Dll/main.c
+++ b/src/REL/m402Dll/main.c
@@ -682,7 +682,12 @@ static void fn_1_1FC4(Vec *arg0, float *arg1, s32 arg2, s32 arg3)
             var_r30 = 1;
         }
     }
+#ifndef MPT_PC
+    /* On MPT_PC this is left out: it adds sp18 to the bytes of the pointer arg0, not to *arg0. On the
+     * console the write lands in a dead stack slot; here it overruns the 8-byte slot, which /GS turns
+     * into a fail-fast (Party Board patch 0024, after Goooooooal's in 0023). */
     VECAdd((Vec *)&arg0, &sp18, (Vec *)&arg0);
+#endif
 }
 
